IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `whsact-dates`

-- Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) -- the worker's questions about being covered, about an incident, about
-- stopping unsafe work, and about what the Act does and does not give a worker.
--
-- SEAT: the worker: the person who carries out work for a business or undertaking in the ACT and is hurt, made ill,
-- or put at risk there, or the family member asking for a worker who died. "Am I a worker?", "Was my injury a serious
-- injury?", "Did the business have to report it?", "May I stop unsafe work, and what then?", "Can I sue under this
-- Act?", "Can my contract take my rights away?", "May the business charge me for safety gear?". The regulator, the
-- inspector and the court get no seat; what they decide is asked as a fact or answered conditionally.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty with a fixed deadline here (written notice of an incident within 48 hours of the regulator's
-- requirement, s 38 (4) (b)) is a regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 5 (6) and s 7 (worker), Part 3 (ss 35, 36, 37, 37A, 38 (4), (7), (9)), Division 5.6 (ss 83-88)
-- with s 103, s 267, s 272, s 273. The claims machinery (Part 6, s 184, Part 12, Part 13, s 34A, Schedule 4) is in
-- whsact-claims.l4. What is out, and why: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT), A2011-35, Republication No 30 (R30), effective 26 November 2025,
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/A2011-35.txt. The Work Health and Safety Regulation 2011 (ACT) is not
-- deposited: where the Act leaves something to it, the answer says so.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to someone else are INPUTS. Answers follow
-- the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): "(the text's no)" marks the text's own no;
-- "NOTHING-kind" marks a limit in the text's own wording, or silence, or a pointer outside the deposited sources.

§ `Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT): the worker's questions`

§§ `s 7 -- Am I a worker`

-- s 5: a person conducts a business or undertaking alone or with others, for profit or not (s 5 (1)), including through
-- a partnership or unincorporated association (s 5 (2)); "A volunteer association does not conduct a business or
-- undertaking" (s 5 (6)). Whether what you work for is a "business or undertaking" is otherwise not defined: asked.
DECLARE `Who I carry out work for` IS ONE OF
    `a business or undertaking, run alone or with others, for profit or not (including a partnership or an unincorporated association)`
    `a volunteer association: volunteers working together for community purposes, none of whom employs anyone to work for it`
    `no one: I carry out no work for a business or undertaking`

-- s 7 (2): a police officer is a worker, and at work while on duty or lawfully performing police functions.
DECLARE `Police officer` IS ONE OF
    `I am not a police officer`
    `I am a police officer, on duty or lawfully performing police functions`
    `I am a police officer, off duty`

DECLARE `Am I a worker` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you carry out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking (s 7 (1)), whether as employee, contractor, labour hire worker, outworker, apprentice, student on work experience, volunteer or otherwise`
    `yes: you conduct the business or undertaking yourself and carry out work in it (s 7 (3))`
    `yes: a police officer is a worker, and is at work now, being on duty (s 7 (2))`
    `you are a worker as a police officer, but not at work while off duty (s 7 (2) (b))`
    `no: a volunteer association does not conduct a business or undertaking (s 5 (6)), so work for it does not make you its worker (the text's no)`
    `no: you carry out no work for a person conducting a business or undertaking (the text's no: s 7 (1)'s "if" fails)`

@export Am I a "worker" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 7)? A worker is anyone who carries out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking.
GIVEN `who for` IS A `Who I carry out work for` @desc Who you carry out work for
      `I run it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an individual who conducts that business or undertaking yourself (alone or with others) and carry out work in it
      `police` IS A `Police officer` @desc Whether you are a police officer, and if so whether you are on duty
GIVETH A `Am I a worker`
`s 7 -- am I a worker` `who for` `I run it` `police` MEANS
    IF `police` EQUALS `I am a police officer, on duty or lawfully performing police functions`
    THEN `yes: a police officer is a worker, and is at work now, being on duty (s 7 (2))`
    ELSE IF `police` EQUALS `I am a police officer, off duty`
         THEN `you are a worker as a police officer, but not at work while off duty (s 7 (2) (b))`
         ELSE IF `who for` EQUALS `a volunteer association: volunteers working together for community purposes, none of whom employs anyone to work for it`
              THEN `no: a volunteer association does not conduct a business or undertaking (s 5 (6)), so work for it does not make you its worker (the text's no)`
              ELSE IF `who for` EQUALS `no one: I carry out no work for a business or undertaking`
                   THEN `no: you carry out no work for a person conducting a business or undertaking (the text's no: s 7 (1)'s "if" fails)`
                   ELSE IF `I run it`
                        THEN `yes: you conduct the business or undertaking yourself and carry out work in it (s 7 (3))`
                        ELSE `yes: you carry out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking (s 7 (1)), whether as employee, contractor, labour hire worker, outworker, apprentice, student on work experience, volunteer or otherwise`

-- Whether the answer is a worker who is (or may be) at work.
GIVEN `the answer` IS AN `Am I a worker`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the answer makes me a worker` `the answer` IF
        `the answer` EQUALS `yes: you carry out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking (s 7 (1)), whether as employee, contractor, labour hire worker, outworker, apprentice, student on work experience, volunteer or otherwise`
    OR  `the answer` EQUALS `yes: you conduct the business or undertaking yourself and carry out work in it (s 7 (3))`
    OR  `the answer` EQUALS `yes: a police officer is a worker, and is at work now, being on duty (s 7 (2))`
    OR  `the answer` EQUALS `you are a worker as a police officer, but not at work while off duty (s 7 (2) (b))`

§§ `Part 3 -- Incident notification: s 36 serious injury or illness`

-- s 36 (1) (b): the listed injuries, each counting "if a person would ordinarily seek immediate treatment".
DECLARE `Listed injury` IS ONE OF
    `the amputation of a body part`
    `a serious eye injury`
    `a serious burn`
    `the separation of skin from an underlying tissue (such as degloving or scalping)`
    `a spinal injury (including a fracture of 1 or more vertebrae)`
    `a fracture of the pelvis, the skull or other facial bones`
    `another serious bone fracture`
    `a serious crush injury`
    `the loss of a bodily function`
    `serious lacerations`
    `none of these`

-- s 36 (1) (d) and (3): "a relevant registered health professional" means a doctor, an enrolled nurse, a nurse, or a
-- registered paramedic (other than a student). "Doctor", "nurse" and "enrolled nurse" are the Legislation Act's terms,
-- asked as facts.
DECLARE `Treatment after a substance exposure` IS ONE OF
    `I was not exposed to a substance, or sought no treatment after it`
    `I sought treatment from a doctor`
    `I sought treatment from an enrolled nurse`
    `I sought treatment from a nurse`
    `I sought treatment from a registered paramedic (not a student)`
    `I sought treatment only from someone else`

GIVEN `the treatment` IS A `Treatment after a substance exposure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `from a relevant registered health professional` `the treatment` IF
        `the treatment` EQUALS `I sought treatment from a doctor`
    OR  `the treatment` EQUALS `I sought treatment from an enrolled nurse`
    OR  `the treatment` EQUALS `I sought treatment from a nurse`
    OR  `the treatment` EQUALS `I sought treatment from a registered paramedic (not a student)`

DECLARE `Serious injury or illness` IS ONE OF
    `a serious injury or illness: it would ordinarily require immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital (s 36 (1) (a))`
    `a serious injury or illness: a listed injury for which a person would ordinarily seek immediate treatment (s 36 (1) (b))`
    `a serious injury or illness: a serious head injury or illness (s 36 (1) (c))`
    `a serious injury or illness: treatment sought from a registered health professional within 48 hours after exposure to a substance (s 36 (1) (d))`
    `not a serious injury or illness under s 36 (1) (a) to (d); a regulation may prescribe others (s 36 (1) (e)) and is not deposited (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 36 (1): a "means" definition, but its para (e) hands further cases to a regulation that is not deposited, so when
-- (a) to (d) all fail the answer is NOTHING-kind, not the text's no (fork F3). A regulation may also take an injury out
-- (s 36 (2)); none is deposited, so the answer is "serious" as far as the Act goes.
-- (c): "a serious head injury or illness, including any head injury or illness resulting from (i) a significant blow,
-- knock or other shock to the head, whether or not immediate treatment is sought; or (ii) repeated blows, knocks or
-- shocks to the head".
-- (d): "within 48 hours after exposure": 48 hours exactly is within.
GIVEN `in-patient` IS A BOOLEAN, `the listed injury` IS A `Listed injury`, `would seek immediate treatment` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `significant blow` IS A BOOLEAN, `repeated blows` IS A BOOLEAN, `other serious head injury` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the treatment` IS A `Treatment after a substance exposure`, `hours after exposure` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Serious injury or illness`
`the s 36 answer` `in-patient` `the listed injury` `would seek immediate treatment` `significant blow` `repeated blows` `other serious head injury` `the treatment` `hours after exposure` MEANS
    IF `in-patient`
    THEN `a serious injury or illness: it would ordinarily require immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital (s 36 (1) (a))`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`the listed injury` EQUALS `none of these`)) AND `would seek immediate treatment`
         THEN `a serious injury or illness: a listed injury for which a person would ordinarily seek immediate treatment (s 36 (1) (b))`
         ELSE IF `significant blow` OR `repeated blows` OR `other serious head injury`
              THEN `a serious injury or illness: a serious head injury or illness (s 36 (1) (c))`
              ELSE IF `from a relevant registered health professional` `the treatment` AND `hours after exposure` AT MOST 48
                   THEN `a serious injury or illness: treatment sought from a registered health professional within 48 hours after exposure to a substance (s 36 (1) (d))`
                   ELSE `not a serious injury or illness under s 36 (1) (a) to (d); a regulation may prescribe others (s 36 (1) (e)) and is not deposited (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Was my injury or illness a "serious injury or illness" under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 36)? A serious injury or illness at work is a notifiable incident that the business must report to the regulator immediately.
GIVEN `in-patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your injury or illness would ordinarily require immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital (whether or not treatment was available or you sought it)
      `the listed injury` IS A `Listed injury` @desc Which of these injuries or illnesses you had, if any
      `would seek immediate treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person with that injury would ordinarily seek immediate treatment (whether or not treatment was available or you sought it)
      `significant blow` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a head injury or illness from a significant blow, knock or other shock to your head (whether or not you sought immediate treatment)
      `repeated blows` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a head injury or illness from repeated blows, knocks or shocks to your head
      `other serious head injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have any other serious head injury or illness
      `the treatment` IS A `Treatment after a substance exposure` @desc Whether you were exposed to a substance and, if so, who you sought treatment from
      `hours after exposure` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after the exposure you sought that treatment (0 if not exposed)
GIVETH A `Serious injury or illness`
`s 36 -- was mine a serious injury or illness` `in-patient` `the listed injury` `would seek immediate treatment` `significant blow` `repeated blows` `other serious head injury` `the treatment` `hours after exposure` MEANS
    `the s 36 answer` `in-patient` `the listed injury` `would seek immediate treatment` `significant blow` `repeated blows` `other serious head injury` `the treatment` `hours after exposure`

GIVEN `the answer` IS A `Serious injury or illness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the answer is serious` `the answer` IF
    NOT (`the answer` EQUALS `not a serious injury or illness under s 36 (1) (a) to (d); a regulation may prescribe others (s 36 (1) (e)) and is not deposited (NOTHING-kind)`)

§§ `Part 3 -- s 35 notifiable incident, s 37 dangerous incident, s 37A sexual assault incident`

-- s 37 (1) (a) to (m). (n) "another incident prescribed by regulation": not deposited.
DECLARE `Dangerous incident kind` IS ONE OF
    `an uncontrolled escape, spillage or leakage of a substance`
    `an uncontrolled fire, implosion or explosion (including an electrical or arc flash explosion)`
    `an uncontrolled escape of gas or steam`
    `an uncontrolled escape of a pressurised substance`
    `an electric shock`
    `the fall or release from a height of a thing`
    `the collapse, overturning, failure or malfunction of, or damage to, plant that must be authorised for use under a regulation`
    `a mobile plant incident (mobile plant overturning, colliding, pinning or ejecting a person, malfunctioning, or moving out of control)`
    `the collapse or partial collapse of a structure`
    `the collapse or failure of an excavation or its shoring`
    `the inrush of water, mud or gas in workings, an underground excavation or a tunnel`
    `the interruption of the main ventilation system in an underground excavation or tunnel`
    `a serious fall (a person falling, or at risk of falling, to a lower level, into a hole, trench, pit, void or water, or onto a dangerous surface or object)`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Notifiable incident` IS ONE OF
    `a notifiable incident: the death of a person (s 35 (a))`
    `a notifiable incident: a serious injury or illness of a person (s 35 (b))`
    `a notifiable incident: a dangerous incident (s 35 (c), s 37)`
    `a notifiable incident: a sexual assault incident (s 35 (d), s 37A)`
    `not a notifiable incident: it did not arise out of the conduct of a business or undertaking (the text's no)`
    `not a notifiable incident under s 35 (a) to (d) as far as the Act goes; a regulation may prescribe further serious injuries or dangerous incidents and is not deposited (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 35: "notifiable incident means any of the following arising out of the conduct of a business or undertaking". A
-- "means" definition; para (b) and (c) carry the regulation's further cases (s 36 (1) (e), s 37 (1) (n)), so the
-- residual answer is NOTHING-kind (fork F3).
-- s 37: "any of the following incidents, in relation to a workplace, that immediately or imminently exposes a person to
-- a serious risk to the person's health and safety".
-- s 37A: "an incident (including a suspected incident) in relation to a workplace that exposes a worker or any other
-- person at the workplace to sexual assault".
@export Is what happened at my workplace a "notifiable incident" that the business had to report to the regulator (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 35 to s 37A)? Notifiable incidents are a death, a serious injury or illness, a dangerous incident, or a sexual assault incident, arising out of the conduct of a business or undertaking.
GIVEN `arising` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident arose out of the conduct of a business or undertaking (for example, out of the work being done there)
      `a death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person died
      `in-patient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone's injury or illness would ordinarily require immediate treatment as an in-patient in a hospital
      `the listed injury` IS A `Listed injury` @desc Which of these injuries or illnesses someone had, if any
      `would seek immediate treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person with that injury would ordinarily seek immediate treatment
      `significant blow` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone has a head injury or illness from a significant blow, knock or other shock to the head
      `repeated blows` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone has a head injury or illness from repeated blows, knocks or shocks to the head
      `other serious head injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone has any other serious head injury or illness
      `the treatment` IS A `Treatment after a substance exposure` @desc Whether someone was exposed to a substance and who they sought treatment from
      `hours after exposure` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after the exposure they sought that treatment (0 if not exposed)
      `the dangerous kind` IS A `Dangerous incident kind` @desc Which of these incidents happened, if any
      `serious risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that incident immediately or imminently exposed a person to a serious risk to their health and safety
      `sexual assault` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there was an incident (or a suspected incident) in relation to the workplace that exposed a worker or another person at the workplace to sexual assault
GIVETH A `Notifiable incident`
`s 35 -- is it a notifiable incident` `arising` `a death` `in-patient` `the listed injury` `would seek immediate treatment` `significant blow` `repeated blows` `other serious head injury` `the treatment` `hours after exposure` `the dangerous kind` `serious risk` `sexual assault` MEANS
    IF NOT `arising`
    THEN `not a notifiable incident: it did not arise out of the conduct of a business or undertaking (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `a death`
         THEN `a notifiable incident: the death of a person (s 35 (a))`
         ELSE IF `the answer is serious` (`the s 36 answer` `in-patient` `the listed injury` `would seek immediate treatment` `significant blow` `repeated blows` `other serious head injury` `the treatment` `hours after exposure`)
              THEN `a notifiable incident: a serious injury or illness of a person (s 35 (b))`
              ELSE IF (NOT (`the dangerous kind` EQUALS `none of these`)) AND `serious risk`
                   THEN `a notifiable incident: a dangerous incident (s 35 (c), s 37)`
                   ELSE IF `sexual assault`
                        THEN `a notifiable incident: a sexual assault incident (s 35 (d), s 37A)`
                        ELSE `not a notifiable incident under s 35 (a) to (d) as far as the Act goes; a regulation may prescribe further serious injuries or dangerous incidents and is not deposited (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 38 -- Duty to notify of notifiable incidents`

-- s 38 (1): the business "must ensure that the regulator is notified immediately after becoming aware". "Immediately"
-- fixes no number, so it is not a WITHIN; the answer says it. s 38 (4) (b): a person giving notice by telephone must, "if
-- required by the regulator, give a written notice of the incident within 48 hours of that requirement being made".
-- Time counted in hours from the requirement.
DECLARE `Business` IS ONE OF `the person conducting the business or undertaking`
DECLARE `Notice step` IS ONE OF `give written notice of the incident`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Business` `Notice step`
`written notice within 48 hours of the requirement` MEANS
    PARTY `the person conducting the business or undertaking`
    MUST `give written notice of the incident`
    WITHIN 48
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person conducting the business or undertaking` BECAUSE "no written notice of the notifiable incident within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement (s 38 (4) (b))"

GIVEN `the hour` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `written notice at this hour is in time` `the hour` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `written notice within 48 hours of the requirement` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the hour`), (EVENT `the person conducting the business or undertaking` `give written notice of the incident` `the hour`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Written notice` IS ONE OF
    `in time: written notice was given within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement (s 38 (4) (b))`
    `not yet given, and the 48 hours have not run out (s 38 (4) (b))`
    `late: no written notice within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement (s 38 (4) (b))`
    `s 38 (4) (b) does not apply: notice was not given by telephone, or the regulator did not require written notice (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export Did the business give written notice of my workplace incident in time (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 38 (4) (b))? A business that notifies the regulator by telephone must, if the regulator requires it, give written notice within 48 hours of the requirement.
GIVEN `by telephone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business notified the regulator of the incident by telephone
      `required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator then required the business to give written notice
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business has given the written notice
      `hours to notice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after the requirement the written notice was given (if not given, how many hours have passed since the requirement)
GIVETH A `Written notice`
`s 38 (4) (b) -- was the written notice in time` `by telephone` `required` `given` `hours to notice` MEANS
    IF NOT (`by telephone` AND `required`)
    THEN `s 38 (4) (b) does not apply: notice was not given by telephone, or the regulator did not require written notice (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `given`
         THEN IF `written notice at this hour is in time` `hours to notice`
              THEN `in time: written notice was given within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement (s 38 (4) (b))`
              ELSE `late: no written notice within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement (s 38 (4) (b))`
         ELSE IF `hours to notice` AT MOST 48
              THEN `not yet given, and the 48 hours have not run out (s 38 (4) (b))`
              ELSE `late: no written notice within 48 hours of the regulator's requirement (s 38 (4) (b))`

-- s 38 (7): "must keep a record of each notifiable incident for at least 5 years from the day that notice of the
-- incident is given to the regulator". The earliest day it may be discarded is the day after the last day.
@export Until when must the business keep its record of my notifiable incident (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 38 (7))? The record must be kept for at least 5 years from the day notice was given to the regulator; this gives the last day of that period.
GIVEN `notice day` IS A DATE @desc The day the business gave notice of the incident to the regulator
GIVETH A DATE
`s 38 (7) -- the last day the record must be kept` `notice day` MEANS 5 `years after` `notice day`

-- s 38 (9) (b): for a sexual assault incident the business "must not give information disclosing the identity of any
-- person involved in the incident when notifying the regulator". A flat negative: JUST FALSE. For other incidents
-- s 38 (9) says nothing: NOTHING.
@export When the business notifies the regulator of an incident I was involved in, may it give information that discloses who I am (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 38 (9))? For a sexual assault incident it must not; it gives only its own name and contact details, a description of the workplace, and whether the incident was reported to police.
GIVEN `sexual assault incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident is a sexual assault incident: an incident (or a suspected incident) in relation to the workplace that exposed a worker or another person at the workplace to sexual assault
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 38 (9) -- may the business disclose my identity to the regulator` `sexual assault incident` MEANS
    IF `sexual assault incident` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Division 5.6 -- Right to cease or direct cessation of unsafe work (this group arises only if you stopped, or want to stop, work because it is unsafe)`

-- s 103: "Nothing in this part applies to a worker who is a prisoner in custody in a prison or police gaol." A flat
-- negative: the text's no.
-- s 84: "A worker may cease, or refuse to carry out, work if the worker has a reasonable concern that to carry out the
-- work would expose the worker to a serious risk to the worker's health or safety, emanating from an immediate or
-- imminent exposure to a hazard." Whether the concern is reasonable is a judgment: asked as "would be found reasonable".
-- Not a worker: s 84 speaks only of a worker and says nothing of others (a scope limit with no "only"): NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `May I stop unsafe work` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may cease, or refuse to carry out, the work (s 84)`
    `no: s 84 does not let you cease work on these facts (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `no: Part 5 does not apply to a worker who is a prisoner in custody in a prison or police gaol (s 103) (the text's no)`
    `s 84 speaks only of workers; it says nothing about you (NOTHING-kind)`

@export May I stop, or refuse to do, work I think is unsafe (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 84)? A worker may cease or refuse work if they have a reasonable concern that doing it would expose them to a serious risk to their health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard.
GIVEN `a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker (you carry out work in any capacity for a business or undertaking)
      `a prisoner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a prisoner in custody in a prison or police gaol
      `concern` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are concerned that doing the work would expose you to a serious risk to your health or safety
      `immediate hazard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that risk comes from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your concern would be found reasonable (an objective judgment: answer as a tribunal or court would find it)
GIVETH A `May I stop unsafe work`
`s 84 -- may I stop unsafe work` `a worker` `a prisoner` `concern` `immediate hazard` `reasonable` MEANS
    IF NOT `a worker`
    THEN `s 84 speaks only of workers; it says nothing about you (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `a prisoner`
         THEN `no: Part 5 does not apply to a worker who is a prisoner in custody in a prison or police gaol (s 103) (the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `concern` AND `immediate hazard` AND `reasonable`
              THEN `yes: you may cease, or refuse to carry out, the work (s 84)`
              ELSE `no: s 84 does not let you cease work on these facts (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

-- s 85: the health and safety representative "may direct a worker who is in a work group represented by the
-- representative to cease work if" the same reasonable concern exists (85 (1)); "must not ... unless the matter is not
-- resolved after" consulting the business and attempting issue resolution under division 5.5 (85 (2)), unless "the risk
-- is so serious and immediate or imminent that it is not reasonable to consult" first (85 (3)); and "cannot give a
-- direction ... unless" trained (85 (6)). From the worker's seat: was the direction I followed one s 85 allows, so that
-- my stopping was "cease work under this division" (s 83 (b))?
@export Was my health and safety representative's direction to stop work one the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 85 allows? If it was, stopping work on it is ceasing work under Division 5.6, with the protection of s 88.
GIVEN `in the group` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in a work group the health and safety representative represents
      `concern` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative was concerned that doing the work would expose you to a serious risk to your health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative's concern would be found reasonable (answer as a tribunal or court would find it)
      `consulted and unresolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before the direction, the representative consulted the business and attempted to resolve the matter under the issue resolution procedure, and it was not resolved
      `too urgent to consult` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk was so serious and immediate or imminent that it was not reasonable to consult first (answer as a tribunal or court would find it)
      `trained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative had completed the initial training prescribed for health and safety representatives (now, for another work group before, or equivalent training under a corresponding law)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 85 -- was the direction to stop work one s 85 allows` `in the group` `concern` `reasonable` `consulted and unresolved` `too urgent to consult` `trained` IF
        `in the group`
    AND `concern`
    AND `reasonable`
    AND (`consulted and unresolved` OR `too urgent to consult`)
    AND `trained`

-- s 86: "A worker who ceases work under this division must (a) as soon as practicable, notify the person conducting the
-- business or undertaking that the worker has ceased work under this division, unless the worker ceased work under a
-- direction from a health and safety representative; and (b) remain available to carry out suitable alternative work."
-- "As soon as practicable" fixes no number: no WITHIN.
DECLARE `How I stopped work` IS ONE OF
    `on my own concern (s 84)`
    `on a direction from my health and safety representative (s 85)`

DECLARE `What I must do after stopping` IS ONE OF
    `tell the business, as soon as practicable, that you have ceased work under Division 5.6, and remain available to carry out suitable alternative work (s 86 (a), (b))`
    `remain available to carry out suitable alternative work (s 86 (b)); you need not tell the business yourself, because a health and safety representative directed you to stop (s 86 (a))`

@export What must I do after I stop unsafe work (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 86)?
GIVEN `how` IS A `How I stopped work` @desc Whether you stopped on your own concern, or on a direction from your health and safety representative
GIVETH A `What I must do after stopping`
`s 86 -- what must I do after stopping work` `how` MEANS
    IF `how` EQUALS `on my own concern (s 84)`
    THEN `tell the business, as soon as practicable, that you have ceased work under Division 5.6, and remain available to carry out suitable alternative work (s 86 (a), (b))`
    ELSE `remain available to carry out suitable alternative work (s 86 (b)); you need not tell the business yourself, because a health and safety representative directed you to stop (s 86 (a))`

-- s 88: "If a worker ceases work under this division, that action does not affect the continuity of engagement of the
-- worker for prescribed purposes if the worker has not unreasonably failed to comply with a direction to carry out
-- suitable alternative work (a) at the same or another workplace; and (b) that was safe and appropriate for the worker
-- to carry out." The "prescribed purposes" are in the Regulation, not deposited. Whether a failure was unreasonable is a
-- judgment: asked. A failure to follow a direction to work that was not safe and appropriate does not count.
DECLARE `Alternative work direction` IS ONE OF
    `the business gave me no direction to do alternative work`
    `I was directed to do alternative work and did it`
    `I did not do directed alternative work that was not safe or not appropriate for me`
    `I did not do safe and appropriate alternative work I was directed to do, and the failure was not unreasonable`
    `I unreasonably failed to do safe and appropriate alternative work I was directed to do`

DECLARE `Continuity of engagement` IS ONE OF
    `protected: stopping work does not affect the continuity of your engagement for the purposes prescribed by the Regulation (s 88)`
    `not protected by s 88: you unreasonably failed to do safe and appropriate alternative work you were directed to do (the text's no: its second "if" fails)`
    `not protected by s 88: you did not cease work under Division 5.6 (the text's no: its first "if" fails)`

@export Does stopping unsafe work break the continuity of my engagement (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 88)? Ceasing work under Division 5.6 does not affect continuity, for the purposes the Regulation prescribes, unless you unreasonably failed to do safe and appropriate alternative work you were directed to do.
GIVEN `ceased under the division` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ceased work under Division 5.6: on your own reasonable concern under s 84, or on a direction under s 85 from your health and safety representative
      `the direction` IS AN `Alternative work direction` @desc Whether the business directed you to do alternative work, and what you did
GIVETH A `Continuity of engagement`
`s 88 -- is my continuity of engagement protected` `ceased under the division` `the direction` MEANS
    IF NOT `ceased under the division`
    THEN `not protected by s 88: you did not cease work under Division 5.6 (the text's no: its first "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF `the direction` EQUALS `I unreasonably failed to do safe and appropriate alternative work I was directed to do`
         THEN `not protected by s 88: you unreasonably failed to do safe and appropriate alternative work you were directed to do (the text's no: its second "if" fails)`
         ELSE `protected: stopping work does not affect the continuity of your engagement for the purposes prescribed by the Regulation (s 88)`

§§ `Division 13.8 and Part 14 -- What the Act does and does not give a worker`

-- s 267: "Except as provided in part 6 ..., part 7 ... and division 13.7 ..., nothing in this Act is to be construed as
-- (a) conferring a right of action in a civil proceeding in relation to a contravention of a provision of this Act; or
-- (b) conferring a defence to an action in a civil proceeding or otherwise affecting a right of action in a civil
-- proceeding". s 260: a civil penalty proceeding "may only be brought by" the regulator or an inspector.
DECLARE `The provision breached` IS ONE OF
    `Part 6: discriminatory, coercive or misleading conduct`
    `Part 7: workplace entry by WHS entry permit-holders`
    `a WHS civil penalty provision (Division 13.7)`
    `any other provision (for example a health and safety duty in Part 2)`

DECLARE `A right to sue under this Act` IS ONE OF
    `yes, for discriminatory or coercive conduct: Part 6 gives a civil proceeding (s 112; see the questions on it)`
    `no: only the regulator or an inspector may bring a proceeding for a WHS civil penalty provision (s 260) (the text's no)`
    `s 267 does not exclude it; whether Part 7 gives you a right is not encoded here (NOTHING-kind)`
    `no: this Act gives you no right to sue for the breach, and does not affect any right you have apart from it, such as a claim in negligence or for workers compensation (s 267) (the text's no)`

@export Does the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) give me a right to sue for a breach of it (s 267)? Apart from Part 6, Part 7 and the civil penalty provisions, the Act gives no right of action and does not affect any right you have apart from it.
GIVEN `the provision` IS A `The provision breached` @desc Which kind of provision was breached
GIVETH A `A right to sue under this Act`
`s 267 -- does the Act give me a right to sue` `the provision` MEANS
    IF `the provision` EQUALS `Part 6: discriminatory, coercive or misleading conduct`
    THEN `yes, for discriminatory or coercive conduct: Part 6 gives a civil proceeding (s 112; see the questions on it)`
    ELSE IF `the provision` EQUALS `a WHS civil penalty provision (Division 13.7)`
         THEN `no: only the regulator or an inspector may bring a proceeding for a WHS civil penalty provision (s 260) (the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `the provision` EQUALS `Part 7: workplace entry by WHS entry permit-holders`
              THEN `s 267 does not exclude it; whether Part 7 gives you a right is not encoded here (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE `no: this Act gives you no right to sue for the breach, and does not affect any right you have apart from it, such as a claim in negligence or for workers compensation (s 267) (the text's no)`

-- s 272: "A term of any agreement or contract that purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of this Act or any
-- duty owed under this Act or to transfer to another person any duty owed under this Act is void."
@export Is a term of my contract or agreement void because it tries to take away the protection of the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) (s 272)? A term that purports to exclude, limit or modify the Act or a duty under it, or to transfer a duty to someone else, is void.
GIVEN `excludes or limits` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of the Act, or of a duty owed under it (for example, "the worker accepts all safety risks")
      `transfers a duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term purports to transfer to another person a duty owed under the Act (for example, making you responsible for the business's duty to keep the workplace safe)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 272 -- is the term void` `excludes or limits` `transfers a duty` MEANS
    IF `excludes or limits` OR `transfers a duty` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

-- s 273: "A person conducting a business or undertaking must not impose a levy or charge on a worker, or permit a levy or
-- charge to be imposed on a worker, for anything done, or provided, in relation to work health and safety." A flat
-- negative: the text's no. A charge for anything else: s 273 says nothing (NOTHING).
@export May the business charge me, or let someone else charge me, for safety equipment, training or anything else done or provided for work health and safety (Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (ACT) s 273)?
GIVEN `a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business (you carry out work in any capacity for it)
      `for safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the levy or charge is for something done or provided in relation to work health and safety (for example protective equipment, safety training, a health check)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 273 -- may the business charge me for safety` `a worker` `for safety` MEANS
    IF `a worker` AND `for safety` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING
